# vehje-ir. Design

The shared program-level representation every framework crate depends on: the
twelve Core substrate forms, the `Family` extension trait plus effect
classification, the grade vocabulary (the four proof axes as concrete runtime
types), one structural-hash definition over IR subtrees, the node builder, the
arena, and interner integration. No algorithms; the data contract the passes
and targets read and write. `#![no_std]`, no alloc: the IR lives in
caller-provided memory, never an ad-hoc heap.

The split against `vehje-signature` is program-level versus language-level.
`vehje-ir` is what a program is: nodes in an arena, families reached through
`Raw`, and the per-program grade annotations. `vehje-signature` is what a
language is: the operation catalogue with its grades and disciplines. Every
per-program pass depends on `vehje-ir`; only the language-build path depends on
the signature.

## The Core substrate (thirteen forms)

Core is the shared evaluation substrate every grammar lowers into: general
evaluation primitives, closed, in the low tens. The thirteen forms are `Lit`,
`Var`, `Let`, `Lambda`, `Apply`, `Project`, `If`, `Match`, `Iter`, `Interp`,
`Raw`, `Handle`, and `Perform`. Eleven are the evaluation shape; the twelfth,
`Handle`, is user-defined resumable algebraic-effect handlers, adopted because it
is a general evaluation primitive rather than a family construct, and because it
unifies the effect set, the host-call boundary, and macro expansion into one
handler discipline. `Raw` is the one typed escape hatch, the seam through which
a family's own node kinds attach to the Core arena. `Match` is Core because
destructuring does not desugar to `If` cleanly; there is no `Seq` because
content is a monoid and concatenation through the child pool suffices.

The thirteenth, `Perform`, is admitted on the same ground as the twelfth: by
algebra, not by census. `Handle` is an eliminator, and nothing among the other
forms built a term it could eliminate, so the handler had nothing to handle. The
free model is generated by the operation symbols and the handler is the
homomorphism out of it, so shipping the homomorphism alone was half an algebra.

`Perform` is deliberately not folded into `Raw`, and the reason is typing rather
than ergonomics. Arithmetic lowers to `Raw`, so performing through `Raw` would
give `1 + 2` an effect, and that is a grade the handler discipline would then
have to subtract everywhere. A future reader tempted to simplify the two into one
should weigh that cost rather than the surface duplication.

The operation identity is a freshly minted `Sym` rather than an id from a fixed
vocabulary, which is what makes labelled `break` work. Two loops in one function
minting their own operations cannot catch each other's exits, labelled `break`
and labelled `continue` need no additional mechanism, and an unlabelled `break`
binds to the innermost enclosing handler because that is the first frame whose
clause list matches.

- `Node`. The Core node, one arena slot. An enum over the thirteen forms; each
  variant carries `NodeRef` operands, a `NodeList` for variadic children, a
  `Sym` for binder identity (the binder positions `Var`, `Let.name`,
  `Lambda.param`), a `Str` for string literals and the `Project` key, a
  `Literal` for literal payloads, a `FamilyId` for `Raw`, and a `ClauseList` for
  `Handle`, as the form requires. Fixed size per slot, stored in a caller-provided node arena,
  addressed by `NodeRef`. `Copy`.
- `NodeRef`. A platform-width index into the node arena (an arvo `USize`
  newtype, not a bare integer).
- `NodeList`. A `{ start, len }` slice into the child-index pool, giving a form
  its variable-arity children (an `Apply`'s arguments, a `Match`'s arms, a
  `Handle`'s clauses) without a per-node allocation.
- `Literal`. The literal payload of a `Lit` node: `Unit`, `Bool`, `Int`, and an
  interned `Str`. Array and blob literals via a byte arena are deferred.
- `FamilyId`. The family identifier for the `Raw` escape hatch, an arvo
  `Uint<16>` newtype.
- `Clause`. One handler clause, state intended: an operation identity, a
  resumption binder, an operand arity, a body, and a span. Each field is
  authored because none is derivable. The arity in particular cannot be
  recovered by counting `Lambda` nesting in the body, because a body may return
  a lambda. Operands curry through the body's lambda chain, reusing binder
  machinery that already works end to end; the resumption does not curry,
  because the check needs a syntactic grip on whether a clause resumes.
- `ClauseList`. A `{ start, len }` slice into the clause region, the way
  `NodeList` slices the child pool.

The `Handle` doc comment lists a clause as carrying an operation, a body, and
the discharge. The third is a correction this round makes: the discharge is which
stage provides the handler, which is the binding-time coordinate the grade
already owns, so authoring it on a clause would author a derived quantity. Two
things are authored.

## The grade vocabulary (the four axes as concrete runtime types)

The graded proof spine lives in the proof and in the coherence argument
(`vehje-check` and the deep dives). At the machine level the axes are concrete
closed enums and fixed-capacity bitmasks, and this crate owns those types
because grade metadata on binder forms is data on existing forms. All four
reuse one bitmask machinery: the `AccessSet` typestate for the type-level sets
and `arvo-bitmask` for the runtime masks.

- Effect. The effect axis is a graded monad, the operations a construct
  invokes. `Effect` is the marker trait for an effect operation (a `'static`
  ZST); an effect set is an `AccessSet` instantiation over `Effect` markers,
  the mirror of the family axis. `EffectMask` is the runtime bitmask of
  inferred operation ids, thermometer-shaped so the lattice join is a bitwise
  OR and inclusion is a subset test. This supersedes the `Pure` / `Reads<E>` /
  `Writes<E>` over `BuildEnv` / `RuntimeEnv` lattice: an effect is now an
  operation the handler discipline services, and the build-versus-runtime split
  moves onto the binding-time axis.
- Binding time. `BindingTime` is the knowledge-source lattice as a closed enum:
  `LanguageAuthor`, `Bundler`, `HostLoader`, `Runtime`. A binding-time grade is
  a join over these (`Knowledge`, a small bitmask), so partial bundling is a
  join rather than a special case, and the two build environments the debt
  reckoning named are two lattice points rather than a marker split.
- Lease. The lease coeffect's grade is a reachability qualifier: `ReachMask`, a
  fixed-capacity bitmask over in-scope binder slots (bounded by the depth cap
  times the per-scope binder width). `Lease` wraps a `ReachMask` as the
  per-value grade. The degenerate immutable strictly-nested depth-lease is a
  `ReachMask` collapsed to lexical nesting, the floor that ships first.
- Assurance. `Assurance` is the assurance lattice as a closed enum:
  `StaticCertified`, `StructurallyCertified`, `ReprovenPerInstance`,
  `DynamicallyChecked`, `Tested`, `Unclaimed`. A property's assurance is a
  projection of its binding-time grade crossed with the property.

The grade metadata a `vehje-check` pass computes lands in `GradeTable`, a
side-table parallel to the node arena keyed by `NodeRef`, exactly as the span
side-table is, so a form carries no grade inline and the annotation is written
by the pass that computes it. Its `get`/`set` are bounds-checked against the
region, returning a `Maybe`-guarded access rather than panicking on an index
past an undersized region, so a table sized smaller than the arena is a
diagnostic, not a crash.

- `Graded`. The witness `vehje-check`'s graded fold returns on success: a
  zero-size proof that the fold ran clean over a given arena and root (no
  dangling child handle). It is the evidence-of-check the witness mint requires,
  so a `Checked` cannot be minted without a clean check having run. Defined here
  because it is the currency between the check pass and the codegen mint, both
  of which name it.

## The arena (no-alloc, caller-provided)

The IR lives in caller-provided bump regions: a node arena (`&mut [Node]`), a
child-index pool (`&mut [NodeRef]`) for variadic children, a span side-table
(`&mut [Span]`) parallel to the node arena, and a clause region
(`&mut [Clause]`, state intended). Each carries a bump cursor; construction
appends and returns a handle. The top-level driver sizes and provides the
regions. No framework library crate allocates.

The clause region is a fourth region rather than a reuse of the two that exist,
and both rejected placements look cheaper than they are. Putting clauses in the
child pool would make one region hold two kinds of thing, since the pool's
element type is `NodeRef` and an operation identity is a `Sym`, so every scheme
that fits one into the other is a pun. Making a clause a non-evaluating `Node`
variant would gain a span and a structural hash for free, and would cost the
property that every term-walking pass currently relies on: resolve, check, ANF,
and encode all iterate a clause list treating each element as a term, and a
non-term in the term arena gives each of them a skip rule.

The cost of the fourth region is real and worth naming rather than eliding: the
host sizes and lends one more buffer, and every signature that threads the arena
threads it too. That is the fourth instance of a cost already paid three times,
and it is what the size-refusing constructors and the `must_use` exhaustion
result exist to make safe.

- `Arena`. Wraps the regions, appends nodes and child lists, and reads them
  back by handle. The `GradeTable` side-table is a parallel region the check
  pass fills.

This is the hand-built equivalent of a cranelift-entity `EntityList` /
`ListPool`. The arena module is kept free of vehje-specific policy so it can be
lifted upstream into hilavitkutin (which owns the memory and storage layer)
once its shape is proven.

## Structural hashing (one definition, three consumers)

`StructuralHash` is a content address of an IR subtree, computed over the arvo
content-hash primitive. It is defined once here because three downstream
mechanisms consume the same identity: hash-consing in `vehje-lower`, dedup
keying in `vehje-fixpoint`'s incremental layer (which takes the content-hash
value, not this type, so it stays `vehje-ir`-free), and the manifest in
`vehje-runtime-gen`. Defining it per consumer would fork the identity primitive
the incremental mechanism's proof-preservation argument depends on. `hash_of`
folds a subtree to its `StructuralHash`, mixing each node's discriminant with
both its children's hashes and its own leaf content: the interned name of a
`Var`, the scalar value of a `Lit`, the key of a `Project`, the payload of a
`Raw`. Folding the leaf content is what makes structurally distinct subtrees
hash distinct (so `Var(x)` and `Var(y)`, or `Int(1)` and `Int(2)`, do not
collide) and equal subtrees hash equal within one interner. The leaf fold mixes
a name's interner handle, which is stable within a single interner but not
across interners, so `hash_of` is sound for within-compilation hash-consing;
cross-interner or cross-artifact identity is served by a byte-image hash
(`vehje-fixpoint`'s `xxhash3_64` over the serialized form), not this word-fold.

## Families and effects

- `Family`. The extension trait. A family is a `'static` type (a ZST marker)
  whose `Debug` rendering carries the human-readable name. `Core` is the
  reference family (its node kinds are the twelve forms); a consumer's families
  are separate, attaching through `Raw`. New families are new types, added
  without touching existing passes.
- The family set. A program carries a type-level set of the families it uses,
  built from `AccessSet` (`Empty` / `Cons<H, T>`) with vehje family markers as
  members. Inclusion against a target's declared set is the `ContainsAll`
  check; the `Contains` diagnostic names a missing family.
- `TargetSets`. The carrier trait a target implements to declare its two
  type-level sets: `type Supports: AccessSet` (the families it handles) and
  `type Permits: AccessSet` (the effects it permits). It lives here, below both
  the check and the codegen crates, so the witness mint in `vehje-check` can
  derive a target's declared sets from the target itself rather than take them
  as free parameters, without a dependency cycle. `vehje-codegen`'s `Target`
  extends it.
- Effect classification. A construct's effect set is the operations it invokes
  that are not locally handled, decidable, never inferred probabilistically.

## The builder (monoid smart-constructors)

- `Builder`. Wraps an `Arena` and exposes one smart-constructor per Core form
  (`lit`, `var`, `let_`, `lambda`, `apply`, `project`, `if_`, `match_`, `iter`,
  `interp`, `raw`, `handle`), each appending a node and returning its
  `NodeRef`. Content composition is monoidal: a sequence of values concatenates
  through the child-index pool rather than a `Seq` node.

## Interner and diagnostics

`Sym` (the generic identity handle from `hilavitkutin-sym`) is the binder
identity in nodes. A source binder name is a `Str` (now a `#[repr(transparent)]`
view over a string-kind `Sym`), so it widens to `Sym` at zero cost via
`Str::as_sym`; a minted binder is a `Sym` from a `Generator<BinderDomain>`.
`BinderDomain` is vehje-ir's `GenerativeDomain` marker (kind `0b001`), exported
so the `Anf` catamorphism can mint fresh binders whose disjointness from source
names is structural (a minted `Sym` is never equal to a string-kind `Sym`).
`Str` remains the identity of string literals and the `Project` key.
`StringInterner<A: ArenaInterner>` interns, with the `ArenaInterner`
impl supplied by the driver. `Span`, `FileId`, `ByteOffset`, `Diagnostic`,
`Phase`, and `Severity` carry the source-location and diagnostic contract;
`Diagnostic` is `Copy` and references only `'static` strings, so the passes
stay alloc-free.

## Non-scope

- Parsers, resolvers, checkers, lowerers, emitters. Each is its own crate; this
  is the data contract they share.
- Consumer family node kinds. They attach through `Raw`; this ships the `Core`
  family only.
- The language-level signature (`vehje-signature`) and runtime execution
  (`vehje-runtime-abi`).

## Dependencies

`hilavitkutin-api` for the `AccessSet` typestate; `hilavitkutin-sym` for `Sym`,
the domain traits, and `Generator` (the binder identity core); `hilavitkutin-str`
for `Str` and `StringInterner`; `arvo` and `arvo-bitmask` for the newtype
discipline, the grade bitmasks, and the content hash; `notko` for `Maybe` and
`Outcome`.
`#![no_std]`, no alloc.
